{ Knight C3, starting the Grand Prix setup. In the actual game: they played D6 right away, so bishop can go to C4. }
2... Nc6 3. f4 { F4, pushing the pawn forward. } 3... d6
{ Black plays d6. As soon as you see d6, you know the bishop can go to c4 and you should be happy. }
4. Nf3 { Knight f3 developed. } 4... g6 5. Bc4
{ Bishop to c4. In the real game, the bishop was later dropped back to b3 proactively so that if black plays d5, white has more options and doesn't have to worry about the bishop being hit. }
5... Bg7 6. O-O { White castles. } 6... Nf6 7. d3 { D3 played. } 7... O-O
{ Black castles. Now white launches the attack! } 8. Qe1
{ Queen to e1! You've seen this idea before. Sliding the queen to the kingside. This was coming - hopefully you knew it. }
8... a6 { Black plays a6. } 9. f5
{ F5! I don't care about the pawn. Not going there to trade pawns - going there to open up the bishop, open the rook, and hopefully open up a diagonal or file to attack the king. }
9... gxf5
{ Black takes the pawn. Now white's bishop is more powerful. The position is opening up for the attack. }
10. Qh4
{ Queen to h4. Now think about the next moves - knight g5 maybe looks good, bishop g5, bishop h6 - lots of ways to attack. Bring the pieces over and start looking for tactics. }
10... fxe4
{ Black captures another pawn - it's a mistake! See if you can find what white plays here. }
11. Ng5
{ Knight to g5! We don't care about recapturing pawns. This creates a triple attack on f7 and a checkmate threat. Black is already in a losing position on move 12 of a 3-minute game. }
11... exd3 { Black tries to stop the immediate checkmate, but white jumps in. }
12. Rxf6
{ Rook takes f6! Sacrificing the rook. We don't care about losing pieces - the only thing we care about is attacking the king and getting checkmate. Black's position is bad - either move the queen and open up to discovered check, or sack the rook. }
12... Bxf6 { Black takes the rook. } 13. Qxh7#
{ Checkmate on h7! Beautiful finish. This demonstrates all the key ideas: Qe1, f5, not worrying about pawns or pieces, bringing pieces in, going for weak spots. That's how you play the Grand Prix Attack. }
